\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Weakest-Link Dominance}
\label{sec:dominance}

Table~\ref{tab:dominance} reports the log-log OLS regression results (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) for all 12 conditions across the 455 panels constructed from the 15 judges in Table~\ref{tab:models}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Bench.} & \textbf{Attack} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Dom.} \\
\midrule
MMLU & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Score manip.   & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
MMLU & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Position bias  & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
MMLU & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
\addlinespace
ARC  & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $-^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Score manip.   & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & n.s.    & $\eta$ \\
ARC  & Position bias  & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
ARC  & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Standardized regression coefficients for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across 12 conditions (455 panels, log-log OLS). $+^{*}$/$-^{*}$: FDR-significant positive/negative (Benjamini--Hochberg, $q < 0.05$); n.s.: not significant. Dom.\ column: $\eta$ = $\eta_{\max}$ sole dominant, $K$ = $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sole dominant, Co = co-dominant \citep{budescu1993dominance}.}
\label{tab:dominance}
\end{table}

The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}$ is the stronger predictor under OLS, reaching FDR significance in 11 of 12 conditions. The sole exception is ARC $\times$ position bias ($p = 0.909$). This near-universal significance is consistent with the prediction of Theorem~\ref{thm:monotonicity}: the most vulnerable panel member is the primary channel through which adaptive attacks succeed.

Panel diversity $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is FDR-significant positive in 7 of 12 conditions and negative in 1 (ARC $\times$ sycophancy), suggesting diversity is predominantly associated with increased vulnerability, counter to the SDPI bound (\S\ref{sec:threat_model}). However, these OLS estimates treat the 455 panels as independent; they are not (each judge appears in 91 panels). Section~\ref{sec:robustness} tests robustness under three corrections. Dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance} yields 4 $\eta_{\max}$-dominant, 7 co-dominant, and 1 $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant conditions. The $r = 0.70$ correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ raises a confound concern; under corrected inference, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s significance drops to 0--1/12.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_robustness_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Significance counts (FDR $< 0.05$) for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across four correction methods over 12 experimental conditions. Wild cluster bootstrap and clustered standard errors are recommended for the 15-judge combinatorial design.}
\label{fig:robustness}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness}

The 455 panels comprise all $\binom{15}{3}$ triples of 15 judges. Each judge therefore appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, and each pair of judges co-occurs in 13. This shared structure induces correlated residuals in the OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}), causing OLS to underestimate standard errors by a factor of approximately $2.5$. Table~\ref{tab:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections that account for this non-independence.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{dir.} & $\boldsymbol{\eta}$\textbf{:}$\boldsymbol{K}$ \\
\midrule
OLS                   & 11/12 & 8/12 & 7+/1$-$ & 1.4:1 \\
Clustered SE (CR1)    & 8/12  & 0/12 & ---     & ---   \\
Mixed effects         & 7/12  & 3/12 & 3+/0$-$ & 2.3:1 \\
Wild cluster bootstrap & 5/12  & 1/12 & 1+/0$-$ & 5:1   \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Robustness of regression results to non-independence correction. Clustered SE uses 3-position max-SE with CR1 correction (13 clusters per position). Mixed effects uses 3-position max-$p$ with random judge intercepts. Wild cluster bootstrap uses $B{=}9999$ resamples, Rademacher weights, and max-$p$ across 3 panel positions. $\eta$:$K$ ratio = $\eta_{\max}$ significant conditions / $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant conditions.}
\label{tab:robustness}
\end{table}

$\eta_{\max}$ remains a robust predictor across all correction methods: 8/12 under clustered standard errors, 7/12 under mixed effects, and 5/12 under the most conservative wild cluster bootstrap. The bootstrap attrition concentrates in ARC conditions (1/6 survive vs.\ MMLU 4/6), likely reflecting lower statistical power in ARC's 2-choice format.

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses under correction (Figure~\ref{fig:robustness}; Appendix~\ref{app:forest_plot}). OLS yields 8/12 FDR-significant conditions (7 positive, 1 negative), but clustered standard errors reduce this to 0/12 and wild cluster bootstrap to 1/12. Point estimates remain predominantly positive, but confidence intervals contain zero in 11 of 12 conditions under bootstrap (Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot}). $\eta_{\max}$ robustly predicts panel vulnerability, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s independent contribution is not statistically distinguishable from zero after correcting for shared judge structure.

The sole negative OLS coefficient (ARC $\times$ sycophancy, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$) does not survive correction (clustered $p = 0.441$, bootstrap $p \approx 0.06$). FDR significance counts are stable across $\epsilon \in \{10^{-3}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-9}\}$, confirming results are not driven by the $\epsilon$ offset in Equation~\ref{eq:regression}.

A simulation-based power analysis (1{,}000 iterations, $\binom{15}{3}$ structure, 13 clusters per position) yields a minimum detectable effect of $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power, below the OLS estimate of 0.164. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s non-significance is thus an \emph{informative null}: any true independent effect is smaller than 0.14 standard deviations. Bias-corrected (CR2) and jackknife (CR3) standard errors corroborate: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ remains 0/12 under both.

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

Panel composition is far more predictive of vulnerability under random faults than under adaptive attack. We simulate non-adversarial evaluation noise by randomly flipping judge votes at rates matching observed per-judge error distributions, then re-run the corrected regressions. Under random faults, both $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ are significant positive predictors of panel error in both benchmark conditions, surviving all correction methods ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$: clustered SE $p = 3.7 \times 10^{-4}$ and $0.009$; bootstrap $p = 0.004$ and $0.019$; $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = +0.215$ and $+0.379$). The regression $R^2$ ranges from 0.73 to 0.81, indicating that panel composition is highly predictable under non-adaptive faults. Under adaptive attack, this predictability breaks down: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s apparent significance is an artifact of panel overlap (0--1/12 after correction), while adaptive adversaries introduce strategic variance beyond what panel structure can explain. The contrast is one of genuineness: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association with vulnerability is real under random faults but inflated under adaptive attack.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_gam_keff_split.pdf}
\caption{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ effect direction split by attack type (GAM with FDR correction). Targeted attacks show positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity increases vulnerability), while shared-bias attacks show negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity provides protection).}
\label{fig:gam_split}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Attack-Type Taxonomy}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM analysis of the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partial effect reveals structure masked by the significance collapse documented in \S\ref{sec:robustness} (Figure~\ref{fig:gam_split}). The 12 conditions split cleanly into two regimes by attack mechanism. \textit{Targeted attacks} exploit judge-specific parsing or format vulnerabilities (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias): the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partial effect is positive in all 6 conditions (2 benchmarks $\times$ 3 attacks). Diverse panels expose more distinct weaknesses for the adversary to exploit. \textit{Shared-bias attacks} exploit cognitive biases common across architectures (sycophancy, verbosity bias, authority bias): the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ partial effect is negative in all 6 conditions. Diverse panels dilute shared susceptibility rather than exposing new attack surface.

This 6:6 split is obscured by linear OLS, which averages over the two regimes. The taxonomy provides mechanistic explanatory power: the \emph{direction} of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect is predictable from attack type, even when its \emph{magnitude} is not statistically significant after correction.

\subsection{Aggregation Rules Modulate the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 10+/1$-$  & 0.89 \\
Majority        & 11/12 & 8/12  & 7+/1$-$   & 0.298 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 9/12  & 9+/0$-$   & 0.284 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 7/12  & 1+/6$-$   & 0.104 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison (15 models, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Methods are ordered by $R^2$ to highlight the vulnerability gradient from threshold (most predictable) to Bayesian (least).}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_aggregation_gradient.pdf}
\caption{Distribution of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficients across aggregation methods. Bayesian aggregation reverses $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ direction (1 positive, 6 negative), contrasting sharply with majority voting (7 positive, 1 negative).}
\label{fig:aggregation}
\end{figure}

The methods form a vulnerability gradient from threshold ($R^2{=}0.89$, compositional factors almost perfectly predictive) to Bayesian ($R^2{=}0.104$, Figure~\ref{fig:aggregation}). Bayesian calibration reverses the diversity channel: the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient direction flips from predominantly positive (7+/1$-$) to predominantly negative (1+/6$-$), and partially weakens $\eta_{\max}$ (11/12 $\to$ 9/12). The directional reversal is clear, but the low $R^2$ (0.104) indicates limited predictive power: Bayesian aggregation decouples vulnerability from composition. Threshold voting amplifies both channels ($\eta_{\max}$: 12/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$: 11/12) because unanimity makes panels maximally sensitive to composition. These results are structurally independent of non-independence: changing the aggregation rule alters the dependent variable, not the panel overlap.

